1. Introduction and problem statement

1.1 Background
Running, walking and cycling events require reliable administration of event details,
registrations, participant records and finishing results. Managing these activities
through disconnected spreadsheets, messages or manual processes can introduce
duplicated entries, inconsistent data and delayed reporting. A web-based solution
offers a central place for organisers to manage events and for participants to interact
with them.

1.2 Problem statement
The project addresses the need for a structured event management platform in
which participants can discover and enrol in events, organisers can control their own
event records, and race results can be recorded and retrieved securely. It must
support role-based access, persistent data, clear navigation and a maintainable
technical design.

1.3 Proposed solution
RaceDay uses an ASP.NET Core MVC user interface that communicates with an
ASP.NET Core REST API. The API applies application rules and uses Entity
Framework Core to access a SQL Server database. Azure Blob Storage stores
uploaded images. The architecture enables independent development and
deployment of the API and web client.

2. Project aims, objectives and scope

2.1 Aim
To design, implement and verify a functional web-based event management system
for participants and organisers, demonstrating contemporary software engineering
practices across the application lifecycle.

2.2 Measurable objectives

 Provide registration, login and logout with Participant and Organizer roles.
 Enable organizers to create, edit, view and manage events they own.
 Allow organizers to define event categories and review enrolments and results.
 Enable participants to browse events, enroll and view their own enrolments and results.
 Persist application data using SQL Server and Entity Framework Core.
 Support profile images and event banners through Azure Blob Storage.
 Expose functionality through documented REST endpoints and a separate MVC client.
 Verify critical behavior with unit tests, manual workflows and GitHub Actions.
 Package the API and MVC applications as Docker images and publish the tested images to

     Docker Hub.

                                                                                                                1
2.3 Project scope

The delivered scope includes event discovery, organiser event administration,
category management, enrolment, result tracking, user profiles, image upload and
presentation, and automated build/test verification. It also includes local container
testing and Docker Hub publication. A public cloud-hosted RaceDay website,
payment gateway, ticket scanning, accommodation booking and other features
discussed for a separate future event platform are not claimed as part of this POE.

