5. System architecture and design

5.1 Architectural overview

The solution separates presentation, application interfaces, shared contracts and
data access. A browser sends requests to the MVC application. MVC controllers use
an HTTP client service to call the RaceDay REST API. The API validates the
request, enforces access controls and interacts with SQL Server via Entity
Framework Core. The API also accesses Azure Blob Storage for media operations.

Browser → RaceDay.Mvc (Razor UI and controllers) → RaceDay.API (REST
controllers and services) → SQL Server / Azure Blob Storage

       5.2 Solution projects  Responsibility
                              HTTP endpoints, application logic, persistence integration and
Project                       media services
RaceDay.API                   User interface, session and API client integration
                              Shared request/response models and DTOs
RaceDay.Mvc                   Automated controller-level verification
RaceDay.Contracts
RaceDay.Api.Tests

                                                                      3
5.3 Design considerations

A layered design limits direct database access from the MVC frontend. Shared DTOs
reduce inconsistencies between the frontend and API. Organiser ownership rules
ensure that users can manage their own events while retaining a read-only overview
of events created by others. The application uses responsive browser layouts,
though a complete device-by-device accessibility audit is not documented here.

6. Database design and data management

6.1 Database

The implemented SQL Server database is RaceDayDB_Part2. The developer
confirmed that it was online and accessible using SQL tooling. The database was
developed using an Entity Framework Core code-first workflow and migrations.

6.2 Principal entities  Purpose                      Key relationship
 Entity                 Role definitions             Referenced by Users
 Roles                  Participant and Organiser    References Roles;
 Users                  accounts                     associated with events
                                                     and enrolments
 Events                 Event details and            Created by an organiser;
 Categories             ownership                    has categories
 Enrolments             Event registration           Belongs to an event
 Results                categories
                        Participant registration in  References participant
                        a category/event             and event/category
                        Finishing data and           Associated with
                        position                     event/participant

6.3 Data integrity

Entity Framework Core maps domain entities to database tables and relationships.
The application has been tested for duplicate enrolment behaviour, returning HTTP
409 Conflict for relevant duplicate requests during earlier development. Database
constraints and API validation complement each other; however, this report does not
reproduce a full SQL schema or assert unverified column definitions.

6.4 Container database access

The SQL Server instance was running on the Windows host and listening on TCP
port 1433. A dedicated SQL login, raceday_docker, was created with permissions on
RaceDayDB_Part2 and tested successfully. Docker containers cannot assume that
Windows integrated authentication or the host name localhost will reach the host
database. The containerised API therefore required an explicit SQL-authentication
connection string supplied at runtime. The final API returned HTTP 200 after the
connection was configured.

