7. Application implementation

7.1 Participant functionality
Participants can sign in and reach a dedicated dashboard that shows available
events and account information. Event cards display event type, date, location,
distance and a link to further details. Participants can enrol in event categories and
view a My Enrolments page with category, enrolment date and status. The UI also
provides a cancellation action. A My Results page displays recorded results,
including finishing position, and a profile area supports account details and picture
uploads.

In the container test, a participant successfully logged in at localhost:7010. The
dashboard showed eight available events. The My Enrolments screenshot displayed
four confirmed enrolments. These values describe the test database at the time of
capture, not a permanent system total.

7.2 Organiser functionality
Organisers have a separate dashboard with summary metrics, an event summary
table and links to My Events, Create Event and participant management. My Events
displays events belonging to the signed-in organiser, with a Manage Event action.
The All Events view lists events created by all organisers but is explicitly read-only
and includes a Created By column. This preserves visibility without granting editing
rights to another organiser’s records.

The verified organiser dashboard showed four events, four upcoming events and six
enrolments for the signed-in organiser. The All Events screenshot listed eight events
across two organisers. These values are demonstration data rather than fixed
system capacities.

7.3 Event and category management
Organisers can create and edit events with name, description, date, location,
distance and event type. The interface supports event banners from uploaded files or
HTTPS image URLs, including preview, replacement and removal actions. Category
management supports event-specific categories used during enrolment.

7.4 Results tracking
Organisers can record and review event results, including finish time and finishing
position. Participants can view their own results through the MVC application. During
development, a mapping issue prevented position from appearing in the participant
view; this was addressed by adding the position to the relevant result view model.

                                                                                                                5
8. Security and access control

8.1 Authentication and authorisation
The system implements registration and login flows, password hashing, role-based
access and ownership restrictions. Participant and Organiser routes expose different
functionality. Protected API endpoints were exercised during development, including
successful authenticated requests and rejected requests for conflicting or
unauthorised actions.

8.2 Secrets and configuration
Azure storage credentials and database credentials must not be embedded in
source code, committed to Git or baked into published Docker images. During
verification, the Azure storage connection string was present in local .NET user-
secrets but not in the container runtime; this initially caused HTTP 500 responses.
The required configuration was supplied to the container at runtime, after which the
next failure exposed the separate database connection requirement. Both
configuration issues were addressed without changing the published application
feature set.

8.3 Deployment considerations
The ASP.NET Core Data Protection logs warned that keys inside the container may
not persist when it is destroyed. This is acceptable as a development warning but
should be addressed for long-lived production deployments by persisting and
protecting the key ring. Production deployment would also require TLS termination,
managed secrets, least-privilege database permissions, durable storage and
operational monitoring.

9. Azure Blob Storage integration

The API contains a BlobStorageService that reads AzureStorage:ConnectionString
from configuration and uses separate containers for profile pictures and event
banners. The project uses private blobs and SAS URLs to allow controlled image
retrieval. Profile uploads and event banner operations were implemented and tested
during development.

The service is configured through AzureStorage:ProfilePicturesContainer and
AzureStorage:EventBannersContainer, with project defaults of profile-pictures and
event-banners. The developer verified that all three AzureStorage settings existed in
local user-secrets. The runtime configuration issue discovered in Docker reinforced
the distinction between a development secret store and the environment variables
available inside a container.

