10. API design and integration

10.1 Endpoint groups            Representative operations
 Area                           Register, login, logout, current-user
 Authentication                 identity
                                Get/update profile; upload profile picture
 Profiles                       List/get event; create, update, delete;
 Events                         banner operations
                                Get event categories; create and delete
 Categories                     Create enrolment; get own enrolments;
 Enrolments                     get event enrolments
                                Create result; get own results; get event
 Results                        results

10.2 HTTP conventions

The API uses standard HTTP verbs to expose operations and status codes to
indicate results. Verified development examples include HTTP 200 for successful
requests, HTTP 201 for creation and HTTP 409 for duplicate or conflicting
operations. Swagger was used to inspect and test endpoints during development.
The final Docker API verification called GET http://localhost:7009/api/events and
received HTTP 200.

10.3 MVC API client

The MVC application registers a typed HTTP client, RaceDayApiClient. Its base
address is read from ApiSettings:BaseUrl, with a local fallback in Program.cs. In a
container environment, the base URL must point to the reachable API address rather
than an inaccessible localhost endpoint inside the MVC container. The successful
role-based MVC demonstrations indicate that the client-to-API connection was
working during the local container test.

                                                                            7
11. Testing and verification

11.1 Testing approach

Verification combined automated unit tests, local Release builds, API requests,
browser-based role walkthroughs, SQL connection checks, GitHub Actions execution
and Docker runtime checks. This provides evidence across individual components
and integrated user journeys, though it does not replace a formal performance or
penetration test.

11.2 Automated tests

The RaceDay.Api.Tests project uses xUnit. The most recent reported Release run
passed four tests with zero failures: two category-controller tests and two events-
controller tests. GitHub Actions also completed successfully on the POE branch. Test
names and full console output should be attached from the repository or the final
build log if the rubric requires line-by-line test evidence.

