15. Evaluation against objectives

The delivered solution meets its core stated objectives in the demonstrated
environment: distinct user roles, event and enrolment workflows, results
management, relational persistence, image storage integration, REST
communication, automated tests and container packaging. Docker runtime
verification and Docker Hub publication extend the POE beyond a local IDE
demonstration.
Remaining limitations include the absence of verified public hosting, formal load
testing, independent security assessment and a completed presentation recording.
These do not negate the verified functional outcomes but should be disclosed clearly
in an academic evaluation.

16. Conclusion and recommendations

RaceDay demonstrates the practical integration of backend development, browser-
based MVC presentation, database management, cloud object storage, testing,
version control, continuous integration and containerisation. The project successfully
supports participant and organiser workflows and has been tested through both
conventional local development and Docker-based execution. The successful
publication of the API and MVC images provide an independently identifiable
deliverable on Docker Hub.
The immediate next steps are documentation finalisation and recording the required
demonstration, not adding new features. For a future production release, priorities
would include managed secret storage, persistent Data Protection keys, secure
hosting and TLS, automated deployment, logging and monitoring, and a broader
quality assurance plan.

